% The paper headers
\markboth{IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON WIRELESS COMMUNICATIONS,~Vol.~XX, No.~XX, Month~2026}%
{Cheng \MakeLowercase{\textit{et al.}}: Progressive Digital Semantic Speech Communication for Packet-Loss Channels}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Non-terrestrial networks (NTNs), together with their integration with terrestrial infrastructure, support speech services in remote, offshore, moving-platform, and emergency-response scenarios, where bandwidth is scarce, packet loss is prevalent, and real-time operation is required. In this regime, conventional speech codecs become inefficient at low bitrates, while existing speech semantic communication methods still struggle to support low-bitrate transmission, packet-loss robustness, and low-latency deployment within one framework. To address this problem, we propose Progressive Digital Semantic Speech Communication (PDSSC). PDSSC uses a layered discrete representation that transmits semantic content first and progressively supplements higher-layer detail, thereby supporting progressive transmission under low-bitrate constraints. Our method treats bitrate reduction and packet loss as a speaker-related recovery problem, addressed by a low-step generative recovery model leveraging same-speaker side information for effective low-cost recovery. A lightweight bitrate controller is also introduced for flexible deployment. Experimental results on LibriSpeech show that the proposed method achieves better perceptual quality than Opus while reducing transmission bitrate by 87.5\%, and exhibits stronger robustness under packet-loss conditions. A complexity and latency analysis further confirms that the proposed framework remains practical for real-time deployment.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Semantic communication, low-bitrate speech transmission, progressive transmission, packet-loss recovery
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{N}{on}-terrestrial networks (NTNs) and their integration with terrestrial infrastructure are widely regarded as a key enabler for extending communication services beyond conventional ground coverage and toward ubiquitous connectivity~\cite{3gpp_ntn,cheng2022sagin,ngmn_ntn}. In many NTN-enabled applications---including remote and offshore operations, maritime communications, moving platforms, and emergency response---speech remains a primary real-time service, yet the underlying links are often bandwidth limited, packet switched, and subject to frequent packet loss. These constraints make it challenging to simultaneously ensure intelligibility, naturalness, robustness, and low end-to-end latency, particularly in the ultra-low-bitrate regime.

This challenge stems largely from a mismatch between link conditions and existing speech coding paradigms. Conventional speech codecs, e.g., advanced audio coding (AAC) and Opus~\cite{iso_aac,valin2012opus}, are mature and highly optimized, but they are primarily designed for faithful signal reconstruction and perceptual quality preservation. In the ultra-low-bitrate regime, they may still allocate a non-negligible portion of the rate to fine-grained acoustic details that contribute less to intelligibility, and their quality degrades further when packets are erased. Semantic speech communication provides an alternative perspective~\cite{qin2021semcom,nguyen2025survey} by prioritizing meaning- and task-relevant information, rather than treating all signal components as equally important for transmission.

Recent studies have advanced semantic speech communication from several angles, yet none simultaneously meet the requirements of ultra-low bitrate, packet-loss robustness, and real-time deployment. DeepSC-style approaches~\cite{weng2020deepscs,xie2021deepsc,weng2022deepscst} and DSST~\cite{xiao2023dsst} demonstrate the promise of semantic-aware transmission, but their continuous latent representations are not well aligned with digital, packetized links. It is worth clarifying the positioning of these schemes relative to ours: JSCC-style methods transmit continuous latent representations over analog-like channels whose impairments manifest as additive noise, whereas the digital packetized links considered in this work require discretizing the source into integer symbols, with packet erasure---rather than noise---as the dominant impairment. The two families therefore operate under different channel models and robustness measures, so our quantitative comparisons focus on digital codecs and semantic schemes within the same packetized regime. To better match practical links, low-bitrate methods increasingly adopt learned discrete representations, including neural codecs such as EnCodec~\cite{defossez2023encodec} and Descript Audio Codec~\cite{kumar2023dac}, as well as RVQ-token-based digital semantic schemes~\cite{chen2025rvqgan}. While these improve digital compatibility and reconstruction quality at low rates, the transmitted tokens are typically not ordered by semantic importance, which limits further bitrate reduction and prevents progressive transmission. Meanwhile, receiver-side generative restoration methods can better infer missing information by leveraging advances in neural speech generation~\cite{vanoord2016wavenet,kong2020hifigan} and diffusion-based modeling~\cite{ho2020ddpm,popov2021gradtts}, including codec-based zero-shot generation~\cite{wang2023valle}; representative systems include LargeSC~\cite{tian2025lsm} and packet-loss-focused designs such as Glaris~\cite{han2025esc}, but they usually rely on heavier models and longer inference, making low-latency deployment challenging. Overall, achieving high intelligibility and perceptual quality at very low bitrates remains difficult~\cite{xin2024bigcodec}, and current systems provide limited support for progressive transmission.

To address these gaps, we propose Progressive Digital Semantic Speech Communication (PDSSC), a digital semantic speech communication framework tailored for bandwidth-constrained, packet-switched speech links. On the transmitter side, PDSSC adopts a semantic-priority layered discrete representation: the lowest layers convey intelligibility-critical content, and additional layers progressively refine speaker traits, prosody, and other residual acoustic details. This hierarchy allows both active bitrate reduction (by truncating higher layers) and passive packet erasure to be modeled as the same phenomenon at the receiver---namely, missing high-layer speaker-related information. Motivated by many practical scenarios with relatively stable user populations, PDSSC further leverages a receiver-maintained same-speaker reference library as side information, enabling a low-step generative recovery model to restore the missing detail. Finally, a lightweight automatic bitrate controller is integrated to support flexible operation under time-varying link conditions. Experiments demonstrate that PDSSC achieves strong perceptual quality and robustness in low-bitrate and packet-loss regimes while remaining practical for real-time deployment.

The main contributions are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
\item A progressive digital semantic transmission framework for low-bitrate speech communication, built on a semantic-priority layered discrete representation in which the first layer carries intelligibility-critical content and context while the higher layers progressively encode speaker-related paralinguistic and residual acoustic detail, thereby enabling further bitrate reduction and progressive transmission within a single digital packetized system.
\item A two-layer factor-specific distillation mechanism that explicitly drives the first RVQ layer toward a pretrained semantic teacher and the second RVQ layer toward a pretrained timbre teacher, so that layerwise semantic and speaker disentanglement emerges from the interplay between factor-specific distillation and residual quantization rather than from architectural duplication, producing a transmission-priority-ordered representation within one codec.
\item A unified low-step generative recovery framework that leverages same-speaker side information to restore the missing high-layer detail caused by both active bitrate reduction and passive packet erasure; by starting the recovery path from an already informative incomplete latent rather than pure noise and conditioning on a mixed reference embedding together with time and bitrate, the model recovers speaker-consistent detail on top of the preserved semantic backbone in only a few inference steps, keeping recovery cost low enough for real-time operation.
\item A lightweight channel-adaptive bitrate controller that adapts the number of transmitted layers to the estimated packet-loss rate and the available bitrate budget in real time; because the codec and recovery model are frozen after training, the end-to-end rate-distortion tradeoff is a stable surface that can be characterized once by sampling the frozen pipeline, so a small policy network suffices to select the operating bitrate without retraining the system, improving bandwidth efficiency under time-varying link conditions.
\end{itemize}

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section~II presents the system model and formulates the problem. Section~III details the proposed PDSSC framework. Section~IV reports the experimental results and analysis. Section~V concludes the paper.

